  "notes": "West leads the ♥Q against 3NT. South might as well hold up the ♥A for 3 rounds. Even after this hold up, it is too dangerous to play on diamonds. It is very likely that the defense will be able to win the ♦A and run the heart suit.\n\nSouth should play on clubs, finessing West for the ♣K. It looks very natural to start that suit with the ♣Q. South will be sorry, however, if West was dealt a singleton ♣K. East's club holding will be promoted into a stopper.\n\nSouth should instead play the ♣2. If West has the singleton ♣K, the ♣Q, ♣J, and ♣10 will take care of the rest of East's clubs.\n\nIf West plays low on the ♣2, South plays the ♣10 or ♣J from dummy. South can cross to his hand in spades and play the ♣Q through West. As long as West has no more than 3 clubs to the ♣K, South cannot be prevented from running that suit. If West refuses to cover the ♣Q, the ♠A will be an entry to dummy's clubs.",
  "commentary_paragraphs": [
    "West leads the ♥Q against 3NT. South might as well hold up the ♥A for 3 rounds. Even after this hold up, it is too dangerous to play on diamonds. It is very likely that the defense will be able to win the ♦A and run the heart suit.",
    "South should play on clubs, finessing West for the ♣K. It looks very natural to start that suit with the ♣Q. South will be sorry, however, if West was dealt a singleton ♣K. East's club holding will be promoted into a stopper.",
    "South should instead play the ♣2. If West has the singleton ♣K, the ♣Q, ♣J, and ♣10 will take care of the rest of East's clubs.",
    "If West plays low on the ♣2, South plays the ♣10 or ♣J from dummy. South can cross to his hand in spades and play the ♣Q through West. As long as West has no more than 3 clubs to the ♣K, South cannot be prevented from running that suit. If West refuses to cover the ♣Q, the ♠A will be an entry to dummy's clubs."
